      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To review the outcome of attempted salvage atherectomy performed following failed balloon angioplasty at a single centre."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "All patients undergoing percutaneous directional atherectomy shortly after failed coronary or peripheral balloon angioplasty at St Paul's Hospital, Vancouver, British Columbia, are reported."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Salvage atherectomy was performed in six patients following failed balloon angioplasty prior to hospital discharge. In each case, a discrete mural flap resulted in a compromised lumen and ischemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Percutaneous directional atherectomy successfully recovered atheromatous intimal fragments with restoration of arterial patency in all patients. Media was identified in one specimen and adventitia in none. Perforation did not occur and there were no complications. One patient had documented restenosis and a second had a significant lesion at a more proximal site with a follow-up of four to 15 months (mean nine). Both patients underwent uncomplicated repeat balloon angioplasty without angiographical dissection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Salvage atherectomy has a limited--but still useful--role in the management of discrete, obstructive arterial dissection flaps complicating balloon angioplasty."
        }
      ]},
